@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmt:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/traditions/> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

#
# prima-materia — the Upaniṣadic tradition
#
# Held to the one claim that is attested at an exact place: prāṇa as that on
# which everything else in the person rests. The later technical apparatus of
# channels and centres belongs to other files and other traditions.
#

pmt:Upanisadic a pm:Tradition ;
    rdfs:label "Upaniṣadic tradition"@en, "Upaniṣadische Überlieferung"@de ;
    skos:definition "The transmission of the Upaniṣads as the speculative layer of the Vedic corpus."@en ;
    skos:note "Edition finding, prima-materia#45. Chāndogya 7.15.1 carries the edition it was read from and was reopened in the Sanskrit for that purpose. For 1.11.5 the edition is not recoverable and is left unstated rather than guessed."@en ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad" .

pmc:Prana a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "Prāṇa"@en, "Prāṇa"@de ;
    skos:definition "Vital breath, presented as that on which the parts of the person rest and out of which beings come forth, in the image of a wheel resting on its hub."@en ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Upanisadic ;
    dcterms:source "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 7.15.1 (Sanskrit; received prapāṭhaka-khaṇḍa numbering)", "Chāndogya Upaniṣad 1.11.5" .

#
# The four quarters of the self
#
# Entered against prima-materia#434. The awareness axes in
# ontology/consciousness.ttl deliberately carry no coordinate for the fourth,
# because a coordinate system that took one tradition's doctrine as one of its
# points would be that tradition's doctrine and not a coordinate system. The
# doctrine itself belongs here, with a passage and an edition.
#
# Edition note, and it is a finding of its own: the passages could not be
# taken from the obvious place. Müller did not translate the Māṇḍūkya, and
# Sacred Books of the East vol. XV does not contain it, so the unsourced
# citations that stood in the ontology file could not have come from there.
# The text was opened in Hume's translation, which does contain it.
#

pmc:FourfoldSelf a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The self in four quarters"@en, "Das Selbst in vier Vierteln"@de ;
    skos:definition "The claim that this self has four fourths, enumerated in order: the waking state, outwardly cognitive; the dreaming state, inwardly cognitive; the deep-sleep state, in which the sleeper desires no desire and sees no dream; and the fourth."@en ;
    skos:note "The three named states are given by what the sleeper or waker cognizes, which is the same criterion the sensory-anchoring axis uses and the reason three of its values can be recognised in them. The fourth is given differently, by negation, and that difference is the whole point of the passage."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Upanisadic ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad 2-6, in Robert Ernest Hume (trans.), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads (London: Humphrey Milford, Oxford University Press, 1921), pp. 391-392" .

pmc:Turiya a pm:Conceptualizing ;
    rdfs:label "The fourth"@en, "Das Vierte"@de ;
    skos:definition "The fourth quarter of the self, given by negation throughout: not inwardly cognitive, not outwardly cognitive, not both, not a cognition-mass, neither cognitive nor non-cognitive; unseen, ungraspable, without distinguishing mark, unthinkable, not to be designated. Its positive terms are the cessation of development, tranquil, benign, without a second. The passage closes by identifying it with the self and saying it should be discerned."@en ;
    skos:note "What the verse does not say is as important as what it says, and it is why this node exists rather than a coordinate on an axis. The text enumerates the fourth alongside the other three as a fourth quarter. That it underlies them, is their substrate, or is what the other three are appearances of, is the reading of Gauḍapāda's Kārikā and of Śaṅkara after him, and is not asserted here on the strength of this passage."@en ;
    skos:note "Nothing in the verse gives a criterion by which a reader could decide of a described condition that it is this one. Every mark is a denial, and the one positive phrase, without a second, is a claim about reality rather than about what the practitioner meets. A coordinate has to be decidable from a text; this is not, and that is the second reason it is a claim here and not a value there."@en ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:textualAttestation ;
    pm:withinTradition pmt:Upanisadic ;
    dcterms:source "Māṇḍūkya Upaniṣad 7, in Robert Ernest Hume (trans.), The Thirteen Principal Upanishads (London: Humphrey Milford, Oxford University Press, 1921), p. 392" .
